{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And he said</b> — This was Ashpenaz. He was a saris (eunuch), and he was the officer over all the sarisim (eunuchs) who served [the king] and his wives. And it is written in the words of Ezekiel: והיו סריסים בהיכל מלך בבל (and there shall be eunuchs in the palace of the king of Babylon)." "<b>Rav</b> — Great, like קרית מלך רב. Therefore Yefet said: that they are Daniel and his companions, and his proof is that it says מזרע המלוכה. And he did not speak correctly, for the verse reads: ויהי בהם מבני יהודה, and others מזרע המלוכה, who were sarisim, but regarding Daniel you will not find that he was a saris; rather it is written of him and his companions ילדים, אין בהם כל מום, and there is no blemish worse than that of a saris. And the king did not command to teach Daniel and his companions sefer, which is Chaldean script and language, but only that they stand before the king and not before his wives. And if a disputant claims: if your words are true, that they were not sarisim, why did the king appoint over them the prince of the sarisim? The answer: because the king commanded to seek from Israel youths, good of appearance, and the wicked are known to this day for lying with males. And furthermore: if the youth is castrated, his intellect is diminished, and the king seeks them to be those who stand before him, knowing every wisdom, as I have explained. And Rabbi Joshua said: the word sarisim in the Holy Tongue has two meanings: one, castrated ones; the second, attendants. And שר הסריסים was the prince over the king's attendants, and likewise עם הסריסים והגבורים — there is no need for the sarisim to have been officers over the mighty men, because they were King David's servants, and he did not castrate them; rather he took them captive and they were guards of his wives, as is the way of kings to this day. And likewise: ואת נשי המלך ואת סריסיו. And it is written: ומן העיר לקח סריס אחד אשר הוא פקיד על אנשי המלחמה." "<b>And the word ha-partemim</b> — is foreign and is not found in Scripture except in the scroll of Esther. Perhaps it is Chaldean or Persian, like saganim."], :book :daniel, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Daniel - Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/daniel"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:18:55.339368465Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}